      "op": "replace",
      "path": "/spec/definitions/@D001/definition",
      "value": "For an extended metric space X and A⊆X, diam(A)=sup{edist(x,y):x,y∈A}, with diam(∅)=0. On an ordinary metric space edist is the usual distance embedded in [0,∞], and unbounded sets have diameter ∞. On a bounded set in an ordinary metric space the diameter is a nonnegative real. For every nonnegative integer m, Euclidean space ℝ^m has distance (sum_{j=1}^m (x_j-y_j)^2)^(1/2); ℝ^0 is a singleton. All Euclidean sets below use this metric."
